# taf.ul.status_watchdog

[![Documentation](https://docs.taf.ute.nsn-rdnet.net/projects/taf-ul-status_watchdog/badge/)][1]
[![Requirements](https://taf.wrgitlab-pages.int.net.nokia.com/miscellaneous/static-badges/requirements.svg)](requirements/base.txt)
[![build status](https://wrgitlab.int.net.nokia.com/TAF/taf.ul.status_watchdog/badges/main/pipeline.svg)][2]
[![coverage report](https://wrgitlab.int.net.nokia.com/TAF/taf.ul.status_watchdog/badges/main/coverage.svg)][2]
[![latest release](https://wrgitlab.int.net.nokia.com/TAF/taf.ul.status_watchdog/-/badges/release.svg)][3]


Allows background watching of statuses on BTS during test case execution



## Supported Python versions

Library should work properly with Python versions specified within
`python_requires` option in [setup.py](setup.py).

## Installation

Generic library installation process is described in [TAF user guide][4].

## Usage

First, import `status_watchdog` class and create it's instance in Robot file:

```robot
Library     taf.ul.status_watchdog  WITH NAME  watchdog
```

Then prepare configuration of BTS to which requests for current statuses will be send (it should contain all data required to connect to given BTS). Currently following BTS configuration classes are supported:
* ClassicalBts

If no configuration will be provided then ClassicalBts with default connection details will be used.

```robot
${bts_config}=            ${tl.gnbs[0]}
```

Then initiate background status watching with given time interval between consecutive requests to BTS for current statuses (default value is 2 seconds).
Path to directory where additional logs will be stored can be also provided (by default logs will be stored in CWD).

```robot
watchdog.watch_statuses   bts_config=${bts_config}
...                       interval=5 sec
...                       log_dir='status_watchdog/logs'
```

From now on a background thread will run, periodically check for expected statuses on BTS.
Test case execution will not be blocked by `watch_statuses` keyword - Robot Framework will proceed with remaining part of test case.
If current statuses cannot be read from BTS (i.e. due to it being unavailable) then background status watching will not be stopped.
More details on how `watch_statuses` keyword is collecting unexpected statuses history can be found in Test Automation Framework library documentation: https://taf-ul-status-watchdog.docs.taf.ute.nsn-rdnet.net

Now prepare collection of expected statuses which will be checked on BTS.
Single expected status should be defined by its `component` (runtime distName), `name` and `value`.
Collection of expected statuses should be provided to `expect_statuses` keyword in form of a dictionary: `{component: {name: value}}`
If expected status should have no value (i.e. 'Online' after CNI-61110) then `${None}` must be used.

```robot
&{rmod_statuses}=         Create dictionary
...                       administrativeState=Unlocked
&{nrcell_statuses}=       Create dictionary
...                       blockingState=Unblocked
...                       availabilityStatus=${None}
&{expected_statuses}=     Create dictionary
...                       /MRBTS-1/RAT-1/RUNTIME_VIEW-1/MRBTS_R-1/EQM_R-1/APEQM_R-1/RMOD_R-1=${rmod_statuses}
...                       /MRBTS-1/RAT-1/RUNTIME_VIEW-1/MRBTS_R-1/NRBTS_R-1/NRCELL_R-1=${nrcell_statuses}
watchdog.expect_statuses  statuses=${expected_statuses}
```

Instead of using collection of expected statuses, single status can be set as expected by using `expect_status` keyword.
If expected status should have no value (i.e. 'Online' after CNI-61110) then `value=${None}` must be used.

```robot
watchdog.expect_status    component=/MRBTS-1/RAT-1/RUNTIME_VIEW-1/MRBTS_R-1/EQM_R-1/APEQM_R-1/RMOD_R-1
...                       name=operationalState
...                       value=Enabled
```

At any point in time (between `watch_statuses` and `stop_watching_statuses` keywords usage) `expect_statuses` and `expect_status` keywords can be used to constantly change content of expected statuses list.

To check whether any unexpected statuses were detected `check_unexpected_statuses` keyword must be used. It will raise `UnexpectedStatusesDetected` if any unexpected status has been detected.

```robot
watchdog.check_unexpected_statuses
```

```
UnexpectedStatusesDetected:
2022-02-11 13:27:27.698258 DETECTED component: /MRBTS-1/RAT-1/RUNTIME_VIEW-4/MRBTS_R-4/NRBTS_R-1/NRCELL_R-1, name: blockingState, value: Blocked
2022-02-11 13:27:27.721953 DETECTED component: /MRBTS-1/RAT-1/RUNTIME_VIEW-4/MRBTS_R-4/NRBTS_R-1/NRCELL_R-1, name: availabilityStatus, value: off line
2022-02-11 13:40:40.715675 LOST     component: /MRBTS-1/RAT-1/RUNTIME_VIEW-4/MRBTS_R-4/NRBTS_R-1/NRCELL_R-1, name: blockingState, value: Blocked
2022-02-11 13:40:45.722025 LOST     component: /MRBTS-1/RAT-1/RUNTIME_VIEW-4/MRBTS_R-4/NRBTS_R-1/NRCELL_R-1, name: availabilityStatus, value: off line
```

Status watching will not be stopped even if `UnexpectedStatusesDetected` is raised - it is possible to catch this exception and continue with watching statuses.

```robot
Run keyword and ignore error  watchdog.check_unexpected_statuses
```

Status watching should always be stopped, otherwise connection to BTS will not be closed, sub-thread will not be joined with main thread and remain active in operating system after test case execution is finished. This can be done by using `stop_watching_statuses` keyword (preferably in `[Teardown]` section).

```robot
[Teardown]  watchdog.stop_watching_statuses
```

More information about other available keywords can be found in Test Automation Framework library documentation: https://taf-ul-status-watchdog.docs.taf.ute.nsn-rdnet.net

